      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/392fab62a445326d26f7c637735cb03f13d94139ec0141d1802c8617020b1489.png)\n\nConsider the vector $a,$ shown above. Which of the following could be $a+(-a)?$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $-a.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head is the same point, the tail of $a.$ \n\nThat vector is called the zero vector, and we write\n\n$$\na+(-a)=0.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/96172072cf76df18a675eb506207756e65b7458e2f3c5fa056f54a81335fc6de.png)",
